Name: sql

Type: keyword

Syntax: sql [date]

Summary:
Specifies a <format> for the <date> <function> and the <convert>
<command> that SQL databases use for their DATETIME and TIMESTAMP
columns.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux, html5

Platforms: desktop, server

Example:
put the sql date into tTimestamp -- for example "2026-10-24 14:30:05"

Example:
local tWhen
put the date && the long time into tWhen
convert tWhen to sql date

Example:
convert tTimestamp from sql date to long date and long time

The result:
You can use the modifiers <long>, <short>, <abbreviated>, <english> and
<system> before <sql> in the <convert> <command> without causing a
<error|script error>, but they have no effect on the resulting format.

Description:
Use the <sql> <keyword> to work with dates and times in the form SQL
databases use for their `DATETIME` and `TIMESTAMP` columns:

    YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS

The year has four digits; the month, day, hours, minutes and seconds
have two each. The time is in 24-hour format, and there is no time
zone: the time is local time, as `the sql date` gives it and as the
<convert> <command> reads it. `sql` and `sql date` are the same format.

Converting `from sql date` also accepts a date alone (`YYYY-MM-DD`, as
in an SQL `DATE` column), which is taken to be midnight local time.
Anything else, or anything after the time, is refused: the value is
left as it was and <result|the result> is "invalid date".

A date in this format is recognized only where `from sql date` names
it: as before, `convert "2026-10-24 14:30:05" to seconds` and
`"2026-10-24 14:30:05" is a date` do not recognize it.

<sql> is not a reserved word: a <variable>, a <handler> or a
<custom property> named `sql` works as it always has. It is read as
this <format> only in `the sql date` and in the <convert> <command>'s
formats.

The <sql> format comes from HyperXTalk, so that stacks written for it
work unchanged. `the sql time` is not part of it.

